/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:706:%{\it La stratification du sch\'ema de Hilbert des courbes rationelles de $\PP ^n$ par le fibr\'e tangent restreint},
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:714:%Curve implicitization using moving lines}, Computer Aided Geometric Design 11 (1994), 687-706.
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:748:\begin{prop}\label{Projection} Let $D\subset \PP^{k+1}$ be a rational curve of degree $d\geq 2k$ which is contained in a rational normal surface $S\subset \PP^{k+1}$ and it is as in Conjecture \ref{Conjecture} i) or ii). Let $C\subset \PP^2$ be the projection of $D$ from $k-1$ points outside $S$. Then the splitting type of $C$ is $(k',d-k')$, with $k'\leq k$.
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:756:  Let us consider also how can we relate the splitting type of a rational curve $C$ with its singularities.
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:761:\begin{prop} Let $C\subset \mathbb{P}^2$ be a rational curve of degree $d$, then $C$ has a point of multiplicity $m$ if and only there is a primitive syzygy $(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2)$ of lenght 2 (i.e. such that the $\beta_i$'s are linearly dependent) and degree $d-m$ for a parameterization $(f_0,f_1,f_2)$ of $C$. 
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:764:Notice that with {\it primitive syzygy}, we mean that the syzygy $(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2)$ cannot be written as $\rho(\beta'_0,\beta'_1,\beta'_2)$, where $(\beta'_0,\beta'_1,\beta'_2)$ is a syzygy of smaller degree.
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:769:\begin{prop} Let $C\subset \mathbb{P}^2$ be a rational curve of degree $d$ and splitting type $(k,d-k)$, with $k<[\frac{d}{2}]$. Then for any point $P\in C$ of multiplicity $m$, we have either $m=d-k$ (in this case $C$ is Ascenzi) or $m\leq k$.  
/tmp/tmp.UUvHxzpvVr/_k,d-k_8Luglio.tex:773:$P$ has multiplicity $m$ for $C$ if and only if (see \cite{refAs1}) there is a parameterization $(f_0,f_1,f_2)$ for $C$ such that there is a syzygy of lenght two and degree $d-m$ for the ideal  $(f_0,f_1,f_2)\subset K[s,t]$; here lenght two means that the syzygy has the form $(\alpha_0,\alpha_1,0)$ .  If $d-m=k$ then $C$ is Ascenzi, by definition; on the other hand, if $m\neq d-k$, but $C$ is Ascenzi, there is a different point $Q\in C$ with $\mu_C(Q)=d-k$, so $\mu_C(P)\leq k$. If $C$ is not Ascenzi, the syzygy $(\alpha_0,\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ of minimal degree has length 3 (i.e. the $\alpha_i$'s are linearly independent) and then all the syzygies of degrees $k+1,...,d-k+1$ are of type $\gamma(\alpha_0,\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$, with $\gamma \in k[s,t]_j$, $j=1,...,d-2k+1$,  so the $\gamma \alpha_i$'s are independent,  hence none of these syzygies can be associated to a multiple point, yielding: $d-m\geq d-k$, i.e. $m\leq k$. 
